I know exactly why it is still doing that. This is a very notorious quirk with CSS Flexbox.
When you use my: "auto" (margin top/bottom auto) on an item inside a flex container, it forces the item to stay vertically centered. **However, if the card becomes taller than the screen, the "auto" margin pushes the top of the card *up and out* of the scrollable area**, cutting it off and making it impossible to scroll down!
To fix this permanently, we need to stop trying to vertically center the card. We will tell it to start from the top (justifyContent: "flex-start") and just give it a normal bottom margin so the scrollbar works properly.
### The Fix
Open your JournalStyle.js file, find the **5. Action Screen** section, and replace those two lines with this:
```javascript
  // 5. Action Screen
  actionScreenBox: { 
    p: 5, 
    flexGrow: 1, 
    display: "flex", 
    flexDirection: "column", 
    alignItems: "center", 
    justifyContent: "flex-start", // <-- Forces content to start at the top
    animation: "fadeIn 0.3s ease-in-out", 
    backgroundColor: "#f8fafc", 
    overflowY: "auto" 
  },
  actionCard: { 
    mt: 2, // <-- Normal top margin
    mb: 6, // <-- Extra bottom margin so it doesn't hug the line when scrolled
    p: 4, 
    width: "100%", 
    maxWidth: 650, 
    minHeight: 480, 
    border: "1px solid", 
    borderColor: "divider", 
    borderRadius: 3, 
    backgroundColor: "white", 
    boxShadow: "0 4px 6px -1px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.1)" 
  },

```
### Why this works:
By removing my: "auto" and adding justifyContent: "flex-start", the content will now naturally flow downwards. When you click "Override Schedule" and the date picker appears, the card gets taller, pushes down, and the overflowY: "auto" will let you scroll perfectly to the bottom with 6 units of spacing (mb: 6) at the end.
